{% set local_problem %}
<p>Madison's mature tree coverage means gutters fill up fast. Pine needles accumulate year-round — not just fall. Oak and maple leaves blanket everything from October through December. And with 55+ inches of annual rainfall, clogged gutters don't just look bad — they actively damage your home.</p>

<p>When gutters overflow, water pours down your siding (leaving stains), pools around your foundation (causing cracks and settling), and seeps into places it shouldn't (hello, basement moisture). Madison's clay soil makes this worse — water doesn't absorb, it just sits there, finding the path of least resistance into your crawlspace.</p>

<p>Foundation repair in Madison runs $10,000-30,000 depending on severity. Annual gutter cleaning costs maybe $300-400 total. We've seen homeowners skip cleanings to save money, then spend $15,000 fixing foundation cracks. Penny wise, pound foolish.</p>

<p><strong>Honest truth about DIY cleaning:</strong> Most homeowners miss the downspouts. They scoop debris out of the gutter troughs, feel good about it, and don't realize the downspouts are still partially clogged. Water backs up during heavy rain, overflows, and they can't figure out why. We flush every downspout with water until it runs clear. That's the step most DIY cleanings skip.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Our gutter cleaning isn't just scooping debris. Here's the actual process:</p>

<p><strong>Step 1: Hand removal.</strong> We remove all debris from gutter troughs by hand — leaves, pine needles, roof grit, decomposed organic material, whatever's in there. We don't just blow it onto your lawn (you've seen those guys). We bag it and haul it away.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Downspout flush.</strong> Every downspout gets flushed with water until it runs clear. This is critical. Downspout clogs are the #1 thing homeowners miss when cleaning their own gutters. Partial clogs restrict flow and cause backup during heavy rain.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Inspection.</strong> While we're up there, we check for: loose or missing hangers, seam separations, small leaks, rust spots, fascia damage, proper slope. We document anything that needs attention and let you know what we find.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Cleanup.</strong> When we're done, your property looks better than when we arrived. No debris on the lawn, no mess on the driveway.</p>

<p><strong>What we won't do:</strong> We won't tell you your gutters are "fine" if they're not. We see problems constantly that previous cleaners ignored — and we'll be straight with you about what's actually going on up there.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've cleaned gutters across <a href="/recent-projects/">Madison neighborhoods</a> for over 20 years. We have the right equipment, the experience to work safely and efficiently, and we know what to look for beyond just debris.</p>

<p>As a local company based 10 miles away, our reputation matters. We're not a franchised "gutter cleaning" operation that's actually a pressure washing company adding gutters to their service list. This is what we do.</p>

<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> If you hire someone for gutter cleaning and they don't flush your downspouts, you're not getting a complete service. Ask specifically. Some companies skip this step because it takes extra time. We don't.</p>

<p><strong>Our recommendation:</strong> For most <a href="/recent-projects/">Madison homes</a>, schedule cleanings in late November (after leaves drop) and late April (after spring pollen/seeds). If you have significant pine coverage, add one in August. Three cleanings a year keeps everything flowing.</p>
